public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // If n is less than or equal to 0, it cannot be a power of 3
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Check if n is divisible by 3
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3; // Divide n by 3
        }

        // If n is still greater than 1 after division, then it must be a power of 3
        return n == 1;
    }

}
```

This function first checks if `n` is less than or equal to zero, since any number less than or equal to zero cannot be a power of three. Then, it uses a loop to repeatedly divide `n` by 3 until it becomes 1. At this point, `n` must have been a power of three because 3 raised to some power will always result in a value that is also a power of three. The final check at the end ensures that `n` was reduced to 1 during the loop, indicating that it was indeed a power of three.

